  "review": "# Independent inline source/service review\n\nReviewed `6faada402bfb10dec33899bc0d605237a033e96e8d48d97650f9f7830c098d23` with owner-move correction `546690e717fcd7513004e77bc048fb81830c2375a6cbd9685693f9d5ccf3076d`. No concrete production defect found in this bounded closure.\n\nIndependently compiled the exact frozen metadata/source-owner closure using Rust 1.90 and immutable `ad67f3d7…` for existing value/public support types. The current metadata/owner bodies are copied source and newly compiled; this is not a claim that an old provider implements the new private Core bridge. All **123 tests passed, one ignored**, including four independent controls; strict standalone Clippy passed with warnings denied. Parent owns private Core/service validation.\n\nAll 116 candidate payloads, three correction payloads and 15 original-source corpus payloads passed hash verification. All nine candidate pure transforms plus the move correction were independently applied to immutable committed Root inputs and formatted with Rust 1.90; each matched its frozen output exactly. The author recorded f850 as HEAD while two input files already contained mounted changes. Their explicit hashes match committed **e9b7cb9**, which is the complete immutable baseline actually used by this review. No unrelated working-tree state is required.\n\n## Source ordering and partial errors\n\nThe original `processBlockData` pushes its computed row before awaiting the transaction upsert. Only that upsert rejection is caught. It then awaits a distinct `saveMaxHeight` factory, MaxHeight write and indexExists write. The continuation retains these separate PCs and the original DB handle versus height DB handle. A failed raw-row upsert therefore still returns its computed transaction and reaches height writes; a failed first/second height write propagates while retaining earlier writes. The existing owner-helper tests compare complete outcome, ordered dependencies and real native rows for each write-failure ordinal, not just a success flag. Save-height and genesis controls preserve their separate failure prefixes; full retained Main startup/catchup callers are not claimed by this live-block component.\n\nReexecuted all **13 unchanged original-source scenes /38 records** under both genuine numeric profiles. Both byte checks passed with original fixture `d492fdfc…` unchanged. This source corpus is evidence for actual caught errors, pending applied returns and static/instance caller boundaries; it is not represented as 13 new Rust Main matches.\n\nAn extra independently executed raw height `{toString:0}` control checks a subtle catch boundary. Key interpolation fails inside the original upsert try; its catch log interpolates the same height and throws again, so no height write occurs. Both genuine numeric profiles return the same TypeError. The copied old owner and new continuation agree on the complete outcome/dependencies/native rows and zero native writes. An exploratory expectation that the catch would continue was disproved by the unchanged source; its failing log is retained, and no production change was proposed.\n\n## Actual append versus source return\n\nThe immutable native plan retains original method/arguments, exact Before/complete-KV Post, page directory and actual owner-bound append. Preparing performs no write. Append does not call the source resolver. Only the retained original source call is settled after complete Post/directory verification. The tests explicitly hold that return after actual append and verify that repeated advance neither decodes again nor writes MaxHeight/indexExists. Scope substitution is rejected without changing the retained frame.\n\nThree new independent real-filesystem controls pass:\n\n* A foreign same-namespace/sequence plan is rejected before any IO; both its Post resolver and append refuse, and the original plan remains usable on its real owner.\n* An additional actual metadata row after append makes the old complete-Post proof fail even though MaxHeight still has the requested value. Failed resolve/reappend leaves the later pin and IO counters unchanged.\n* A duplicate insert plan with no native mutation still cannot enter append under an installed cold capability. The refusal precedes IO; after releasing that exact scope, it returns its original duplicate error without a write.\n\n`append_inline_database` checks the cold-write guard first, then snapshot/context/full-KV Before, then the existing escrow-aware actual append. Existing copied tests also cross a real publication with a sealed escrow permit and reject it without that permit. This review does not infer durability/authentication from those digests.\n\n## Same owner and old mode\n\nThe Core frame holds source locals/one prepared native plan, not another Runtime or Ledger. It moves the same TxIndex owner out only for immediate backend evaluation and restores it before returning an await. Source resume uses that retained owner/scope and does not reevaluate the business method. The new service entry has an explicit profile/head domain. The old entry passes false; conditional field visitors leave its old stream unchanged, and its existing metadata/owner bodies gain includes only.\n\nThe correction explicitly moves `inline_profile` through all four no-index extraction/restoration sites and adds the corresponding carrier field. Its derived output exactly matches the frozen correction. This is field preservation only: the old WAL remains on the old active entry, and these moves do not advertise inline-plan recovery or widen historical/closed readers.\n\nRemaining component boundaries are concrete: the direct inline entry has no new WAL mode/reopen implementation; cross-publication append needs the actual escrow permit; retained startup/catchup/historical playback and process-loss behavior are not completed by this package. Applied bytes never stand in for Promise completion. These explicit gates are consistent with the inspected API; they are not counted as success.\n\nNo Root files, frozen stages, Git state or financial snapshots were changed. Parent's two unrelated cold-prefix review assertion compile fixes (`matches!` instead of `assert_eq!` on non-PartialEq metadata errors) preserve the same exact error variants and were accepted; they do not change this review's production scope.\n",
